00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.860
So, of course. Leave it to the good readers of

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:36.600
dank memes and reddit to take this already absurd trend and blow it absolutely out of proportion.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.780
In an effort to take this to the absolute max,

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.220
they created the ultimate Bee Movie mod, and I quote:

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:48.760
"One: The entire Ice Age pentology but every syllable is replace with the entire Toy Story trilogy...

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:54.300
but Two: every second that the color green is in frame it is replaced with every video ever uploaded on Youtube...

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.020
but Three: every 10 seconds every episode of the simpsons plays...

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.620
but Four: every word with a vowel is replaced with the Bee Movie...

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:07.340
but Five: every time a bee is shown it is replaced with every episode of SpongeBob played backwards."

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.680
Stupid?! Of course it is.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.100
Funny? Well I thought so.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.220
But what really got me was that in the picture they have

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.740
this huge number for the runtime in there for comedic effect.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.240
And after I gave this post one enormously sarcastic eye roll,

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:25.000
It got me thinking: how long would this video actually be?

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.960
It seems like an impossible question. I mean,

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.600
look at some of the things you have to solve for.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.160
But "impossible" is my middle name.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.520
Matthew Robert Danger Impossible Patrick.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.900
And sure, there are some answers in these reddit threads, but um...

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:41.020
I did that math, and they are completely wrong. (SHOTS FIRED!!!!)

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.800
So just like a Lannister always pays his debts,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.800
so does a theorist always show his work.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.700
And today is dedicated to determining the length of the longest, memeist video ever conceived

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.320
Sharpen those #2 pencils for some of the dankest math you've ever seen.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.520
So right now, you might be reacting like this guy:

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:00.920
"Algorithms!"

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:03.160
"Math!"

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:04.160
But here's the good news:

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.260
although this sounds really complicated, the math

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:09.800
we can use to solve it all isn't hard. It's just a bunch of multiplication.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.940
Since we're just replacing something from one show

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.340
or movie with something else, if we can calculate

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:17.160
the number of times each replacement action happens,

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.640
we can use good ol' multiplication to go from the inside out.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.840
Parentheses within parenthesis within parenthesis.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.600
Parentheses, for when math gets META.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.960
So as long as we're keeping track of our units, we should be fine.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.960
The bad news though is that we're going to be getting into the territory of really scarily large numbers.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.500
But hey, you can put a lot of big numbers into the TI 83

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.020
Silver edition, of course, so we should be fine.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.500
Growing up we didn't have enough money to buy the

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.600
Silver edition calculator, and I was always really

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:43.840
jealous of anyone who had it.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.780
Talk about your #FirstWorldProblems.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:50.380
Hey, but real talk, that would come preloaded with games, which was pretty sweeeeet!

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:52.140
Now starting at the end of the prompt and working our

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.100
way backwards makes the most sense because it has the simplest parameters.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.180
So first we turn to the overlord of memedom himself:

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.800
(Shrek, no wait) Spongebob Squarepants.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.880
The original meme stipulates that the episodes of

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:04.540
Spongebob be played backwards, but as long as we're

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.460
playing them at a standard speed, it's not going to

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.100
change how long they are, so its an easy number to find.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.440
At the time of this recording, we've been asked about the resident of a

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:12.920
"pineapple under the sea"

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.740
205 times in all.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.880
Take out all the commercial breaks, and each episode

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:20.980
and each episode is going to clock in at about 22 minutes, or 1,320 seconds.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:21.480
(This is just a captionist's rant so pay no attention. But MatPat is forgetting that not all Spongebob episodes are a half hour-shows, some are an hour like Truth or Square)

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:26.300
The total time for the whole series then figures out to be 270,600 seconds, meaning

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.880
that if you can give up sleep, work, school, and the toilet,

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.480
you can actually catch up with the entirety of Spongebob's misadventures

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.300
in a little over 3 days straight.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.340
Spoiler Alert!!! You still won't know the Crabby Patty secret formula,

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:39.400
but you might just get a chuckle out of the fact that

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.320
Mr. Krabs lives in a place called Bikini Bottom.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.480
Get it? Crabs, bikini bottom - edgy. -_-

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:53.180
"The smelly smell. The smelly smell that smells. The smelly." (Spoiler Alert: The smell is anchovies)

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:55.620
And sure. Only 3 days might sound like we're getting off

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.580
to a slow start, but remember, that's three solid days

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:03.240
of SpongeBob for every single instance of the other rules.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:04.340
So are you ready for more math?

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:06.640
"I'm ready, I'm ready, I'm ready."

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:07.340
"MATH!"

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.040
I figured you might be. Let's continue.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.300
The next layer out is the Bee Movie rule.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.480
We play those Spongebob Squarepants rules every time we see a bee in the Bee Movie.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.900
Now in a shocking development, there are actually a lot of bees in the Bee Movie, (-_-)

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.480
but here's where we have to start interpreting the meme a little bit.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:24.740
What does it mean by "every time we see a bee"?

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:29.560
One run-through of Spongebob for every bee in every frame? That seems a bit like overkill.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:30.920
Instead, I opted for a slightly more

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:34.660
conservative approach: Every second a bee was in the frame throughout the movie.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:37.400
The runtime of the Bee Movie, if you cut out all the

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:38.780
credits and the DreamWorks stuff at the beginning

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.960
is 82 minutes and 4 seconds.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.280
Bee Movie without the bees, on the other hand,

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.180
is a poultry 12 minutes and 28 seconds

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.540
meaning there are 69 minutes ( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°) and 36 seconds worth

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:53.220
of pervy bees looking to have an interspecies romance. ( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°)

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:56.420
"I meant, thank you so much again, for before."

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:58.380
"Oh, that? Oh, that was nothing."

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.800
"Well not nothing, but, but anyway."

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.960
Ah! 69 minutes. I see what you did there completely by coincidence, Bee Movie. ( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°)( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°)( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°)

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.980
I mean, what's the deal with bees trying to get it on with human women?

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:14.160
Who thought that would be a good idea at the box office?

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.280
Am I right, Jerry Seinfeld?

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.660
The guy who called YouTube the garbage can of content.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:23.040
It's thanks to us that anyone cares about that stupid movie of yours.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.660
Go have more coffee with with comedians and cars.

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:29.440
Anyway, that puts us at 7136 seconds, or

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:35.000
7136 times we're going to have to play all those Spongebob episodes all the way through.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:40.240
Well, actually, it's 7136 runs of every Spongebob episode for every

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:45.140
one of our next rules: Every single episode of the Simpsons, but every word spoken that contains

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.720
a vowel being replaced with the Bee Movie, which is

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:50.580
yatta, yatta, yatta. That's another Seinfeld thing. You get the point by now.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.540
The Simpsons is currently in the midst of its 28th

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:56.860
season, and has aired 609 different episodes.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:00.740
So how many cromulent words have been in each episode of the Simpsons?

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:02.540
Well, we studied the transcripts of a few different

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:04.960
Simpsons episodes, including ones with wordless

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:07.860
montages like Last Exit to Springfield, as well as those

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.160
with a lot of dialogue, like Homie the Clown.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:13.140
The average number of words per Simpsons script was

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:15.620
about 2145.

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:18.080
And because most words have a vowel in there

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.500
somewhere we just assumed that it was 100%.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:24.580
So, 2145 time 609 episodes means

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:29.000
1.3 million words over the course of those 609 episodes.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.840
In other words, that's 1.3 million times we have to repeat

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:34.620
all the previous conditions.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:35.740
"You have a cow."

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:37.520
And now we get to the big one.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.100
The runtime of all the videos ever uploaded to Youtube.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.720
You'd think as someone who's lived and breathed YouTube

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.720
for years now that I'd have the inside track

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:50.320
on this one, but uh... no. This one I actually have to calculate out.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:52.740
YouTube keeps a lot of stats close to its chest.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.340
But using previously talked-about statistics can give us

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:56.960
a pretty darn good estimate.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.580
Projections from late 2015 claimed that YouTube had

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.240
500 hours of video uploaded every minute.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.480
Which would likely be even higher now with

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:06.980
YouTube's continued growth.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.240
So if you take previous announcements from YouTube on its

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:13.980
yearly upload rates, with my personal favorite being 2012's YouTube April Fool's video,

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:17.100
which stated "an hour of video is uploaded to YouTube every second..."

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:24.620
Wow, an hour a second? Oh my god, it's nearly 10 times that 5 years later. That is insane!

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:26.900
And most of those minutes are Bee Movie memes

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:28.880
or thousand degree knife videos.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:30.200
Ooh, what's it gonna cut through this time?

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:31.680
Oh, I bet it cuts through the shoe.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.000
Yeah, it cut through the shoe.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.080
Ooh, I bet it cuts through the ice.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:36.660
Wow! A hot knife melted ice. -_-

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:38.460
Speaking of thousand degree knife videos, it's like

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:41.880
the only trend that's not being covered in this video.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:43.700
Get with it thousand degree knife videos.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:44.600
Become meme-ish. (1000° Knife vs Film Theory)

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:46.740
Anyway, by using this graph and similar ones that

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.440
list off average bulk time of video uploaded to

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.380
YouTube ever hour broken down by the year, you get

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:58.640
you get over 630 million hours of video that have ever been uploaded to YouTube.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.260
And since we're measuring it in seconds,

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.960
well, yeah you should probably stop thinking about

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:05.080
raw numbers at this point and instead think about

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:11.680
Keyboard Cat. One of the greatest talents to ever appear on this site over those 630 million plus hours.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:14.640
Next step: to Toy Story and beyond.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:17.000
The three Toy Story movies have a total runtime of

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:21.220
4 hours 36 minutes and 40 scenes that kick you right in the feels.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:25.000
So that's a total runtime of 16,561 seconds.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.840
Now, the rule here is that for every one of those seconds

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.900
in which the color green is visible in the frame,

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:34.500
this supersized meme repeats all the previously mentioned steps.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.920
And its here that I have to make a bit of a confession.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:38.740
Forgive me, Internet, for I have sinned.

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:44.960
I didn't study every 397,464 frames of all three Toy Story movies.

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:46.560
Because honestly, that's what it would take with

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:50.660
24 frames per second and 16,561 seconds of movie

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:54.420
it's nearly 400,000 thousand frames to explore.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.360
It just can't be done.

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.940
But what I did do was take selected clips from each

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:01.700
of the three movies, slow them down, and determine

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:04.160
what percentage of time green was visible.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:08.300
It averaged out to be a staggering 84.6% of the time.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:10.280
And it makes a lot of sense when you think about it.

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:16.320
Buzz has green on him. Rex is green. The army men are green. The aliens are green. The furniture's green.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.800
The outdoor scenes with trees and grass are green.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:22.360
Sid's eyes are green. There is a lot of green in these movies,

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:24.720
especially for Pixar. Am I right?

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.360
Cha-ching!

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:27.980
But seriously, that's why they're creating a fourth

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:29.920
Toy Story movie, right? Shameless cash grab?

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:30.940
"I like money."

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:38.820
Anywho, I digress. 16,561 seconds times 84.6% of seconds with green in them rounds out to

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:42.320
14,011 seconds in which the color green is in frame

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.220
throughout the Toy Story trilogy.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:46.720
Ooohhh, we are almost there, guys.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:49.960
And last, but certainly not least, we have the Ice Age movies.

00:11:49.960 --> 00:11:53.000
Every thing we've calculated so far is repeated for every

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.800
single syllable in all five...

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:59.000
Wait, five? There have been five Ice Age movies? 0_0

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:01.320
Man! I missed a couple of those.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.520
Alright, so every syllable in each Ice Age movie forces us to

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:06.500
everything we've calculated so far.

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:08.280
When I first started working on this episode, I thought

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.820
for sure this would be the hardest one to calculate,

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:13.020
but I was surprisingly able to get a pretty exact

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.300
calculation by taking the transcripts, spoken dialogue only,

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:18.300
for each of the five movies, cutting it into

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.560
manageable pieces, and then running it through an

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.100
automated syllable counter.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:24.360
Granted, the syllable counter couldn't recognize a few

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.760
words, like Scrat, but it did give us the otherwise reasonable count of

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:31.120
34,085 syllables across the five movies.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:34.560
Ooohhh, and with that, it's time to recap.

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:38.720
We have the 270,600 seconds of Spongebob footage

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:43.140
repeated 7136 times for every second of

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.560
visible bees in Bee Movie, which is shown once for

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:50.720
every one of the 1,306,105 words spoken with the vowel

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:52.800
of the Simpson's canon... *gasps for breath*

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:53.780
"Ay caramba!"

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:56.480
... which is then repeated every 10 seconds of the

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:00.800
630,720,000 hours of video that have every been

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:05.300
uploaded to YouTube, which rolls 14,011 times due to the

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:08.080
green seconds in Toy Story 1, 2, and 3,

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:12.040
which all plays once for every single one of the

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:16.120
34,085 syllables of the Ice Age pentology.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.980
Make sure you've converted it all to seconds,

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:20.000
multiplied it all the way through,

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:24.540
then convert it to years, and we get the drum roll worthy number of

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:25.420
*Weak drum taps*

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:28.620
I said, the drum roll worthy number of

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:29.620
*More weak drum taps*

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:32.360
God, seriously. Where's the drum roll?

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:35.240
*Indistinct voice* What? Are you kidding me?

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:39.740
Can we at least get like a dramatic horn blow or something?

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:44.220
What? Are you, are you.. fine, fine. If that's the best we can do, fine.

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:47.660
The dramatic, slide-whistle-worthy number of...

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:50.120
*Slide whistle noises* Ahem.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:58.460
8 octillion, 672 septillion, 127 sextillion, 482 quintillion years!

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:01.200
Or just about 8 and a half octillion years if you round down.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:02.940
Now if you're like me, you hear a number like that,

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.560
and you're like "what the heck does that mean?"

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:07.480
And yeah, when you're talking about numbers this huge,

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.220
it gets really hard to wrap your head around.

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:11.560
So let's look at this giant number from a couple different angles.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.340
First, it's worth noting that Earth, as a planet, has

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:18.100
has been around for a little bit more than 4 and a half billion years.

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:22.640
And the whole universe? About 13.7 billion years.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:25.440
Now billion and octillion might both sound like

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:27.520
impossibly large numbers, but trust me.

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:30.020
Octillions are WAY bigger.

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:35.580
If you started playing this video the moment the Big Bang occurred, straight through today,

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:39.600
you would be less than 1% of the way through the YouTube videos,

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:45.640
which would mean you're on the first second of Toy Story, and the first spoken syllable of Ice Age.

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:49.640
Remember that there are thirty-four thousand syllables of Ice Age to get through.

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:53.660
And it took us from the Big Bang to now, and we're still only on the first one.

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:59.780
Scientists estimate that the sun will explode in the next 4 to 5 billion years, taking Earth with it.

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:03.020
So by the time the planet Earth ceases to exist,

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:05.700
this video would still be going on.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:09.380
This is also around the time the universe is supposed to die via heat death

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:14.400
so time and space will come to an end, but bad movie memes will outlive us all.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:19.480
So how do you illustrate a length of time that's truly longer than the lifespan of the universe?

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:22.920
What is the best way to convey to you how long this video is?

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:24.400
Let me put it this way:

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:26.420
Let's say we set up race where one person sets out

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:28.520
out to watch this video from start to finish.

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:33.700
And another person puts all of Earth's water into one giant eye dropper,

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:39.240
and then tries to refill all those bodies of water one drop, or one milliliter at a time.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:42.780
The person with the giant eye dropper would still win if

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:46.760
they only put in one drop, one milliliter of water,

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:50.160
every five thousand years.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:53.380
Ever tried to fill an ocean with an eye dropper,

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:55.760
one drop every five thousand years?

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:57.580
It is gonna take a while.

00:15:57.580 --> 00:15:59.540
And don't even get me started on how long that

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:00.940
damn video would take to buffer. ( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°)

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:01.780
But hey...

00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:04.320
That's just a theory.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.300
A Film Theory.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:08.860
And... cut.( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°)

